  <div class="narrow">
    <span class="label">Frequently asked</span>
    <h1>Common questions</h1>
    <p>
      These come up again and again in our community. Short answers here, and a
      link to the fuller page for each one.
    </p>

    <h2>What is obliterative surgery, and what does colpocleisis involve?</h2>
    <p>
      Obliterative surgery treats prolapse by closing most of the vaginal canal
      so the organs cannot descend, rather than rebuilding the support that
      holds them up. Colpocleisis is the main procedure. The surgeon stitches
      the vaginal walls together in an operation that usually takes about an
      hour. For the right candidate it works very well and regret is rare, but
      it does permanently end vaginal intercourse. That is why it is chosen most
      often by older women who are certain about the trade off.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/treatment/surgery/">Read the full page on surgery</a></p>

    <h2>I am a few days out from a cystocele and rectocele repair. What is normal right now?</h2>
    <p>
      Tiredness, light bleeding or discharge, soreness, and a few emotional
      wobbles are all normal in the first week or two. Walk a little further
      each day, keep your stools soft, lift nothing that makes you strain, and
      put nothing in the vagina until you are cleared. Fever, worsening pain,
      heavy bleeding, or trouble urinating are all reasons to call your team
      today rather than wait.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/treatment/recovery/">Read the full page on recovery</a></p>

    <h2>I keep reading about things I can never do again after surgery. Is any of that real?</h2>
    <p>
      Lifelong bans are increasingly questioned by specialists. Much of that old
      advice was never evidence based, and newer research supports a more
      individual, more liberal return to activity. What counts is your surgeon's
      guidance for your operation, so ask them directly, and be specific about
      the activities that actually matter to you.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/treatment/recovery/">Read the full page on recovery</a></p>

    <h2>Has anyone had prolapse come back after surgery? How long did it take?</h2>
    <p>
      Some anatomical change after a repair is common, but most women never feel
      symptoms again, and only about one in nine need repeat surgery within five
      years. When recurrence does happen it is usually in the anterior, or
      bladder, compartment, and the risk runs higher when the original prolapse
      was advanced. There are always next options.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/treatment/recurrence/">Read the full page on recurrence</a></p>

    <h2>Has anyone had success with bioidentical hormones?</h2>
    <p>
      Low dose vaginal estrogen, on prescription, has good evidence behind it for
      vaginal tissue health and is commonly used alongside prolapse treatment.
      Whether it improves the prolapse itself is still being studied. Compounded
      "bioidentical" hormones are not better proven than the regulated options.
      Individual experiences vary a lot, so this is worth a conversation with a
      menopause informed provider.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/treatment/non-surgical/">Read the full page on non-surgical options</a></p>

    <h2>What are the most common symptoms?</h2>
    <p>
      Pelvic heaviness or dragging, a bulge you can feel or see, symptoms that
      worsen through the day, leaking when you cough or laugh, and trouble
      fully emptying your bladder or bowels.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/symptoms/">Read the full page on symptoms, including the private self check</a></p>

    <h2>Do I need surgery?</h2>
    <p>
      Not necessarily. Many women manage prolapse well for years, or for good,
      with pelvic floor physiotherapy, a pessary, and some changes to everyday
      habits. Surgery is one option among several, not the default.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/treatment/">Read the full page on treatment</a></p>
  </div>
